{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-10949" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of convergence $R$ and interval of convergence $I$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{2}^{n}(x-1)^{n}}{n!}\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-10949" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{2}^{n}(x-1)^{n}}{n!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{2}^{n+1}(x-1)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{{2}^{n}(x-1)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{2(x-1)}{n+1}| \\\\  & =2|x-1|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1} \\\\  & =2|x-1|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCondition of convergence $L<1$ is satisfied for all $x\\in I=(-\\infty ,\\infty )$, which is the interval of convergence in this case. The radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-10949" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "35b1059be1d26e2bb2f9ae24d81c75e5bd115c3601093f1026d2cdfde4c01211"} {"question" "q-10949" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-10949" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$R=2$ and $I=(-1,1)$"] ["text" "$R=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $I=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$"] ["text" "$R=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $I=(0,1)$"] ["text" "$R=\\infty $ and $I=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$"] ["text" "$R=\\infty $ and $I=(-\\infty ,\\infty )$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-10949" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$R=\\infty $ and $I=(-\\infty ,\\infty )$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-13912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of convergence $R$ and interval of convergence $I$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{3}}\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-13912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{3}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{3(n+1)}}{(n+1)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3}}{(x-1)^{3n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{n}^{3}(x-1)^{3}}{(n+1)^{3}}| \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{3}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{3} \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-1{|}^{3} & <1 \\\\ |x-1| & <1 \\\\ -1<x-1 & <1 \\\\ 0<x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $R=1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(0,2).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x-1|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=0$ and $x=2$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=0$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{3}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{3}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3}}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\n- When $x=2$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{3}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(2-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{3}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3}}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis series is convergent by the $p$-series test.\n\nConsequently, the interval of convergence is $I=[0,2].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-13912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "35b1059be1d26e2bb2f9ae24d81c75e5bd115c3601093f1026d2cdfde4c01211"} {"question" "q-13912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-13912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$R=1$ and  $I=(0,2)$"] ["text" "$R=1$ and  $I=(0,2]$"] ["text" "$R=1$ and  $I=[0,2)$"] ["text" "$R=1$ and  $I=[0,2]$"] ["text" "$R=\\infty $ and  $I=(-\\infty ,\\infty )$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-13912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$R=1$ and  $I=[0,2]$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-50871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The power series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{(x-2)}^{n}$ converges at  $x=0.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series converges at $x=1$\n- The series diverges at $x=4$\n- The series diverges at $x=-2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-50871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The center of the expansion is $x=2,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=0,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $2,$ i.e., $R\\ge 2.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[0,4).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[0,4).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/assets/a56e24009403f0177f608ba932c03ab77e8db35e1db3e1221c4fb49efca7219f.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $1\\in [0,4),$ the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $4\\notin [0,4),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is divergent at $x=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $-2\\notin [0,4),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is divergent at $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-50871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "35b1059be1d26e2bb2f9ae24d81c75e5bd115c3601093f1026d2cdfde4c01211"} {"question" "q-50871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-50871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-50871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-50878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The power series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ converges at  $x=-1.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series converges at $x=0$\n- The series converges at $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$\n- The series diverges at $x=2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-50878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The center of the expansion is $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-1,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $\\frac{3}{2},$ i.e., $R\\ge \\frac{3}{2}.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[-1,2).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[-1,2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/assets/03901ede0b4fadb59556c63aa4534b3a9e20c1e8188388e6256434ece03d9211.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $0\\in [-1,2),$ the series is convergent at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $-\\frac{3}{2}\\notin [-1,2),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $2\\notin [-1,2),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is divergent at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-50878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "35b1059be1d26e2bb2f9ae24d81c75e5bd115c3601093f1026d2cdfde4c01211"} {"question" "q-50878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-50878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-50878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-103150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x+3)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-1,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series converges at $x=-5$\n- The series converges at $x=-2$\n- The series converges at $x=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-103150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The center of the expansion is $x=-3,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-1,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $2,$ i.e., $R\\ge 2.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $(-5,-1].$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $(-5,-1].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/assets/7d68a4bd81717c3ad663fc85c45dfce62217a046f683cc92a6869f87ff3b166a.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $-5\\notin (-5,-1],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-5.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $-2\\in (-5,-1],$ the series is convergent at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $1\\notin (-5,-1],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-103150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "35b1059be1d26e2bb2f9ae24d81c75e5bd115c3601093f1026d2cdfde4c01211"} {"question" "q-103150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-103150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"} {"question" "q-103150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1d52fa81aa8ca14f2fca4cfae5a6fd0f7013137d3a178349338cf46ce2b0eb2"}] "usage" {"q-50878" []} "basis" 2467 "retractions" [[17592186176425 :question/problem "The power series $∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}(x - \\frac{1}{2})^{n}$ converges at $x =-1$. Which of the following **must** be true?\n\n1. The series converges at $x = 0$\n2. The series converges at $x =-\\frac{3}{2}$\n3. The series diverges at $x = 2$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186175093 17592186175094 17592186175095 17592186175096 17592186175097] "immutable_fields" [[17592186175092 :answer-field/correct] [17592186175092 :answer-field/id] [17592186175092 :answer-field/key] [17592186175092 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
